(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-macronutrients-micronutrients-water-quiz"]={"title":"Macronutrients, Micronutrients & Water — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the Food and Nutrition Board, what percent of total daily calories should come from protein?","choices":["20% to 30%","45% to 65%","20% to 35%","10% to 15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Food and Nutrition Board sets protein at about 10% to 15% of total calories, with higher-need exceptions such as endurance athletes (up to 1.7 g/kg body weight) and the elderly (1 to 1.2 g/kg). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 86 (RDA for Protein); contrast slide 35 (carbohydrate) and slides 46/66 (lipid)"},{"q":"A fasting blood glucose level greater than 125 mg per 100 mL of blood indicates which condition?","choices":["Metabolic syndrome","Ketosis","Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyperglycemia is defined as blood glucose greater than 125 mg per 100 mL (fasting) — high blood glucose from a lack of glucose control. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32 (Regulating Blood Glucose)"},{"q":"Which body tissue can use only glucose for energy, unlike the brain, which can switch to ketone bodies if necessary?","choices":["Liver","Skeletal muscle","Red blood cells","Adipose tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Red blood cells can only use glucose for energy. The brain prefers glucose but can use ketone bodies (partial fat-breakdown products) when necessary. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 30 (Provide Energy)"},{"q":"Which type of fiber is specifically linked to reducing cholesterol absorption in the gut?","choices":["Functional fiber added to processed foods","Insoluble fiber (e.g., lignin, cellulose)","Soluble/viscous fiber (e.g., pectin, gums)","Resistant starch fiber exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Soluble (viscous) fiber — gum, pectin, carrageenan, mucilage, found in beans, oats, some fruits/vegetables, rice bran, and psyllium — reduces cholesterol absorption and cholesterol synthesis in the liver. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 14 (Fiber: Reducing Cholesterol Absorption); contrast slides 9-10"},{"q":"Consuming more than 60 grams of fiber per day can lead to which problem, according to the lecture?","choices":["Constipation from inadequate fluid intake","Decreased availability of some minerals","Increased risk of colon cancer","Increased cholesterol absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Too much fiber (>60 g/day) requires extra fluid, may decrease the availability of some minerals, and can create unmet energy needs in children. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 16 (Too Much Fiber); contrast slide 15 (Too Little Fiber) and slide 13 (Benefits of Fiber)"},{"q":"A fatty acid with more than two double bonds in its carbon chain is classified as:","choices":["Monounsaturated","Saturated","Trans","Polyunsaturated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Polyunsaturated fatty acids (like omega-3 alpha-linolenic acid and omega-6 linoleic acid) have more than two double bonds in the carbon chain. — Macro/Micro lecture, slides 50-53 (Saturated, Monounsaturated & Polyunsaturated Fatty Acid Structure)"},{"q":"Saturated fatty acids are found primarily in which type of food, and what physical state do they typically take at room temperature?","choices":["Plant foods; solid","Animal fats; liquid","Animal fats; solid","Plant foods; liquid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Saturated fatty acids are found primarily in animal fats and take a solid form at room temperature. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 49 (Fatty Acids and Triglycerides)"},{"q":"Which food source provides the long-chain omega-3 forms DHA and EPA directly, rather than requiring the body to convert them?","choices":["Canola oil, a plant-based source","Chia and hemp seeds, both plant-based","Flaxseed and walnuts, both plant-based","Fatty fish such as salmon and mackerel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fatty fish (salmon, tuna, mackerel, lake trout) and fish oil directly supply DHA and EPA, the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids; about 2 servings of fish per week is the recommended intake. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 56 (Omega-3 Fatty Acid Sources)"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein is described as picking up cholesterol from dying cells, taking it back to the liver, and thereby reducing cardiovascular disease risk?","choices":["Chylomicron","HDL","VLDL","LDL"],"answer":1,"exp":"HDL (high-density lipoprotein) is synthesized by the liver and intestine, is high in protein, picks up cholesterol from dying cells and returns it to the liver, may block LDL oxidation, and reduces cardiovascular disease risk. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 64 (High-density Lipoprotein); contrast slide 63 (Major Lipoproteins in Blood)"},{"q":"A healthy adult who consumes enough protein to exactly replace ongoing protein losses, with no net gain or loss, is in which state?","choices":["Protein equilibrium","Protein-calorie malnutrition","Positive protein balance","Negative protein balance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein equilibrium describes intake that exactly matches protein losses, with no net gain or loss — the state of a healthy adult meeting their needs. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 85 (Protein Balance)"},{"q":"In developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition in children is most closely associated with which outcome?","choices":["A measurable decrease in overall infection risk","A noticeable improvement in overall immune function","Accelerated growth compared to well-nourished peers","Stunted growth and increased risk of infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"In developing countries, protein-calorie malnutrition stunts growth and increases the risk of infection. It's somewhat rare in developed countries, though it's seen in alcoholics and sometimes the elderly. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 87 (Protein-Calorie Malnutrition)"},{"q":"Which vegetarian diet pattern includes plant foods and dairy products, but excludes eggs, fish, and meat?","choices":["Lactovegetarian","Pescatarian","Vegan","Lacto-ovo-vegetarian"],"answer":0,"exp":"A lactovegetarian eats plant foods and dairy products, but no eggs, fish, or meat. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 92 (Food Planning for Vegetarians)"},{"q":"Which nutrient deficiency is a specific concern named in the lecture for individuals following a strict vegan diet?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin A","Fiber intake","Vitamin C"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vegan diets carry deficiency concerns for vitamin B12, vitamin D, calcium, iron, zinc, and omega-3 fatty acids, since B12 in particular comes almost exclusively from animal products. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 93 (Vegan Diets)"},{"q":"Which fat-soluble vitamin is required as a coenzyme in the synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism?","choices":["Vitamin D","Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin K is required as a coenzyme during synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 100 (Vitamins: Classification & Function — Fat Soluble)"},{"q":"Which water-soluble vitamin is required as a coenzyme in the metabolism of carbohydrates and branched-chain amino acids?","choices":["Folate (B9)","Thiamin (B1)","Biotin (B7)","Riboflavin (B2)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thiamin (B1) is required as a coenzyme in the metabolism of carbohydrates and branched-chain amino acids. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 101 (Vitamins: Classification & Function — Water Soluble)"},{"q":"Which statement about vitamin content in fruits and vegetables, a key food source of many vitamins, is accurate according to the lecture?","choices":["Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is ripe","Freezing destroys nearly all vitamin content","Water-soluble vitamins are resistant to destruction by heat and light","Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is unripe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin content is greatest when a fruit or vegetable is ripe, and freezing helps retain nutrients since frozen foods are often blanched first, preserving vitamin content. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 105 (Vitamin Preservation in Foods)"},{"q":"Which of the following is classified as a trace mineral rather than a major mineral?","choices":["Zinc","Magnesium","Sulfur","Calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Zinc is a trace mineral — a component of multiple enzymes and proteins involved in gene expression, protein metabolism, and wound healing. — Macro/Micro lecture, slides 110-111 (Minerals Classification & Function: Major vs. Trace Minerals)"},{"q":"Which of the following is a LOW-sodium food choice, according to the lecture's food-source list?","choices":["Frozen prepared pizza","Store-bought canned soup","Fresh fruits and vegetables","Drive-through fast food"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fresh fruits and vegetables, along with whole grains and meats without sauces, are specifically listed as low-sodium, unprocessed food sources. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 147 (Food Sources of Sodium)"},{"q":"Which mineral is specifically named, along with calcium and magnesium, as emphasized in the DASH diet to help lower blood pressure?","choices":["Iron mineral","Potassium","Copper trace","Fluoride"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DASH diet is high in calcium, potassium, and magnesium, and low in fat and sodium, with an emphasis on fruits and vegetables (naturally low-salt, high-potassium foods) and low-fat dairy. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 158 (Dietary Approach to Stop Hypertension)"},{"q":"What is the primary difference between hard water and soft water, according to the lecture?","choices":["Hard water is unsafe to drink; soft water is always safe","Hard water contains high calcium and magnesium; soft water contains high sodium","Hard water comes only from wells; soft water comes only from municipal supplies","Hard water contains high sodium; soft water contains high calcium and magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hard water contains high levels of calcium and magnesium (soap doesn't lather well in it; 89% of homes have hard water), while soft water contains a high level of sodium (and produces cleaner-feeling clothes). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 140 (What Is the Difference Between Hard and Soft Water?)"},{"q":"What is the Recommended Dietary Allowance for carbohydrate in adults, per the lecture?","choices":["130 grams per day","30 grams per day","500 grams per day","No Recommended Dietary Allowance is set for carbohydrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the Recommended Dietary Allowance for carbohydrate is 130 grams per day for adults. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 35"},{"q":"How many kilocalories per gram do sugar alcohols provide, per the lecture?","choices":["About 7 kilocalories per gram","About 2.6 kilocalories per gram","About 9 kilocalories per gram","0 kilocalories per gram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: sugar alcohols provide about 2.6 kilocalories per gram and are incompletely absorbed. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 27"},{"q":"Per the lecture, fasting hyperglycemia is defined as a blood glucose above what level?","choices":["Greater than 60 grams per day","Exactly 100 milligrams per deciliter in all people","Greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood","Below 40 milligrams per 100 milliliters"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: fasting hyperglycemia is greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture contrast the two forms of digestible starch?","choices":["Amylose is highly branched and amylopectin is straight","Both are forms of nondigestible dietary fiber","Both are simple sugars, not starches","Amylopectin is a highly branched chain of glucose (~80% of starch); amylose is a long, straight chain (~20%)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: amylopectin is highly branched (about 80% of starch) and amylose is a straight chain (about 20%). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 8"},{"q":"According to the lecture, a triglyceride is composed of what?","choices":["Three fatty acids bonded to a glycerol backbone","Two amino acids bonded to a sugar","A single fatty acid attached to cholesterol","A chain of glucose units"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: triglycerides are three fatty acids bonded to glycerol and are the primary form of fat in the body and foods. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 49"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a monounsaturated fatty acid?","choices":["A fatty acid that contains no carbon","A fatty acid with one double bond, such as oleic acid (an omega-9)","A fatty acid with only single carbon bonds","A fatty acid with more than two double bonds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a monounsaturated fatty acid has one double bond, exemplified by oleic acid (omega-9). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 51"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which describes high-density lipoprotein?","choices":["A lipoprotein made entirely of triglycerides with no protein","A carbohydrate transporter in the blood","The most dense lipoprotein, high in protein, that picks up cholesterol from cells and returns it to the liver","The lipoprotein that deposits cholesterol into artery walls"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: high-density lipoprotein is the most dense, high in protein, and returns cholesterol to the liver, reducing cardiovascular risk. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 64"},{"q":"What is the Recommended Dietary Allowance for protein in a healthy adult, per the lecture?","choices":["8 grams per kilogram of body weight","0.08 grams per kilogram of body weight","130 grams per day regardless of weight","0.8 grams per kilogram of healthy body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the Recommended Dietary Allowance is 0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of healthy body weight. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 84"},{"q":"Per the lecture, how are the 20 amino acids that make up proteins classified?","choices":["9 essential amino acids that must come from the diet and 11 nonessential amino acids the body can synthesize","11 essential and 9 nonessential amino acids","All 20 are essential and must be eaten","All 20 can be synthesized by the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: there are 9 essential amino acids (from diet) and 11 nonessential (synthesized by the body). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 72"},{"q":"According to the lecture, water makes up about what fraction of body weight?","choices":["Less than 25% of the body","About 50% to 70% of the human body","About 5% to 10% of the body","About 90% to 95% of the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: water comprises about 50% to 70% of the human body. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 132"}]};
